    <h2 id="plant-density-plants-per-square-foot-and-per-acre">Plant Density: Plants per Square Foot and per Acre</h2>
    <p>
      Spacing and plant density are the same math, read from opposite directions. Spacing tells you the distance between plants; density tells you how many plants fit in a given area. The plant density formula is simple:
    </p>
    <p class="rounded-lg bg-sand-50 px-4 py-3 font-mono text-sm text-bark-700 ring-1 ring-moss-100">
      Plants per sq ft = 1 &divide; (spacing in ft &times; spacing in ft)
    </p>
    <p>
      That&rsquo;s how to calculate plant density from a spacing figure: square the spacing (in feet) and take the reciprocal. The same formula scales up to plants per acre &mdash; multiply by 43,560, the number of square feet in an acre &mdash; though that&rsquo;s rarely what a home garden or landscape bed needs. This plant spacing calculator doubles as a plant density calculator already: switch to square foot gardening mode above and the plants-per-square-foot figure is exactly this number, worked out for you from the spacing chart.
    </p>

    <h2 id="why-tree-spacing-is-different">Trees &amp; Shrubs: Why Spacing Is Different</h2>
    <p>
      Tree and shrub spacing works on a different logic than vegetable spacing, and it's worth understanding before you use the Trees &amp; Shrubs mode above. Vegetable spacing is a seasonal decision &mdash; get it wrong and you fix it next year. Tree spacing is a <strong>permanent decision</strong>. You can't re-space a mature tree once its roots and canopy have filled in; moving or removing an overcrowded tree years later is a far bigger job than pulling up a badly spaced pepper plant.
    </p>
    <p>
      The number that matters is <strong>mature canopy width</strong>, not the size of the sapling you're planting. This is the single most common tree-spacing mistake: a 4-foot bare-root sapling spaced 20 feet from its neighbor looks absurdly over-spaced on planting day, so people plant it closer &ldquo;to fill the gap&rdquo; &mdash; and within a decade both trees are fighting for the same light, water, and root space anyway. Space for the tree it will become, not the tree it is today.
    </p>
    <p>
      Crowded trees and shrubs run into real, long-term problems that don't show up right away: competition for light and water that stunts growth and reduces fruit production, poor air circulation that increases fungal disease pressure (the same airflow principle covered above for vegetables, just at a much larger scale), and structural issues as trunks and branches grow into each other, sometimes requiring corrective pruning or removal years later.
    </p>
    <p class="rounded-lg bg-sand-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-bark-600 ring-1 ring-moss-100">
      <strong class="text-bark-700">Scope note:</strong> the Trees &amp; Shrubs mode above is built for home-garden and small-orchard scale &mdash; the spacing a homeowner planting a handful of fruit trees or a hedge actually needs. It is not a trees per acre calculator for commercial forestry or timber-scale orchard planning, which works at a completely different scale (and with completely different goals, like planned thinning) than a home yard needs.
    </p>

    <h2 id="tree-and-shrub-spacing-chart">Tree and Shrub Spacing Chart</h2>
