    <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-bark-500">
      Ranges reflect real variation by climate, variety, and specific cultivar. The Trees &amp; Shrubs mode above uses a representative default within each range &mdash; adjust the spacing field manually (or choose &ldquo;Custom&rdquo;) if your specific variety or region calls for something different. Our <a href="/calculators/tree-age-calculator/">Tree Age Calculator</a> is useful once trees are established, for estimating age from trunk diameter. For the full tree and shrub care picture &mdash; watering by age, mulching, and feeding &mdash; see our <a href="/clusters/trees-and-shrubs/">Trees &amp; Shrubs guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="worked-example">Worked Example</h2>
    <p>
      You have a <strong>4&times;8 ft raised bed</strong> and want to grow <strong>tomatoes (determinate)</strong> at the recommended spacing of 24 inches in-row and 36 inches between rows. Here&rsquo;s the math:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Plants per row:</strong> 8 ft &divide; (24&Prime; &divide; 12) = 8 &divide; 2 = <strong>4 plants per row</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Number of rows:</strong> 4 ft &divide; (36&Prime; &divide; 12) = 4 &divide; 3 = 1.33 &rarr; <strong>1 row</strong> (floor to whole rows).</li>
      <li><strong>Total plants:</strong> 4 &times; 1 = <strong>4 tomato plants</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Four plants is a reasonable crop for a standard 4&times;8 bed using the row method above. Switch to square foot gardening mode, though, and the count roughly doubles: each tomato takes 4 sq ft, so 32 sq ft &divide; 4 = 8 plants. That's not because the plants need less room in SFG mode — it's because SFG uses the entire bed area without row paths between plants, while the row method above set aside space for a 36-inch aisle. The calculator handles both methods automatically.
    </p>
    <p>
      Need to prep the soil first? Use the <a href="/calculators/raised-bed-soil-calculator/">raised bed soil calculator</a> to figure out how many cubic feet of mix you need before you plant.
    </p>

    <h2 id="common-mistakes">Common Mistakes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Planting at transplant size, not mature size.</strong> A 4-inch tomato seedling looks tiny at 24-inch spacing. Plant it anyway. The spacing is for the mature plant, not the seedling you can barely see in week one.</li>
      <li><strong>Ignoring between-row spacing.</strong> Most gardeners use in-row spacing only and crowd rows together. The result is poor airflow down the row and fungal problems by midsummer. Both dimensions matter.</li>
      <li><strong>Using the same spacing for every crop.</strong> A universal 12-inch spacing is fine for mid-size crops but disastrously tight for squash and far too loose for carrots. Always use crop-specific numbers.</li>
      <li><strong>Mixing up row garden and square foot gardening math.</strong> Row garden spacing includes path space between rows; SFG doesn&rsquo;t. Using row spacing numbers in an SFG grid leads to overcrowding.</li>
      <li><strong>Forgetting to account for paths and borders.</strong> The calculator assumes the full bed area is planted. If you leave a border or a central stepping path, deduct that area from your dimensions first.</li>
      <li><strong>Planting the whole bed edge to edge.</strong> Leave at least <strong>18 inches</strong> of pathway space somewhere in the layout &mdash; between beds, down the middle of a wide bed, or along one side &mdash; so you can actually water, weed, and harvest without stepping on your plants or compacting the soil you just worked to loosen. It&rsquo;s tempting to count every square foot as growing space, but a bed you can&rsquo;t comfortably reach isn&rsquo;t one you&rsquo;ll maintain well.</li>
    </ul>

